Ladybird holds a page’s requests at startup until the background
pages of the extensions that may block them have loaded, or 10 seconds
pass. Firefox does the same through the persisted and primed blocking
listeners of its EventManager (startupBlocking in ext-toolkit.json),
waiting for each such extension’s background to start before letting the
request through; Chromium has no such wait for MV2 webRequest.

Its WebSocket events follow Firefox’s (type websocket; onBeforeRequest
may cancel; onBeforeSendHeaders and onSendHeaders carry the handshake’s
headers) and Chromium’s WebRequestProxyingWebSocket.
